# Generate annotations for one module, from its chase skeleton, its
# source, and its callers.
#
# Invoked from Haskell as a typed function. The schema below drives
# structured output via Grace's toJSONSchema; the model cannot return
# malformed shapes.
#
# Three changes after measuring a local 7B on Pelotero.DB.Pool.
#
# The SOURCE is in the prompt (2026-09-26). The skeleton has no function
# bodies, so a model asked for behavioural facts from signatures alone
# has nothing to draw on and restates the type: nine invariants of the
# form "acquire creates a connection pool using the provided DBConfig",
# twice through a retry loop that told it not to. With bodies in front of
# it, the same model produced "calls 'runSession' with 'pool' and
# 'TxS.transaction TxS.ReadCommitted TxS.Write tx'".
#
# The CALLERS are in the prompt, derived from a project-wide reference
# scan rather than guessed. Asked to produce this field, the model
# returned module names (Hasql.Pool, System.Environment), because the
# %uses lines were the only names it had.
#
# The model no longer emits consumes at all. It is computed.
#
# The examples below are real invariants from chase's own bundle, kept
# because they make the contrast between a fact and a restatement
# explicit.

\arguments ->

let key = arguments.key
let bundle = arguments.bundle
let source = arguments.source
let callers = arguments.callers
let modName = arguments.modName
let driftWarnings = arguments.driftWarnings

let driftSection =
      if length driftWarnings == 0
      then ""
      else "
        Your previous attempt produced these warnings. Fix exactly these
        and keep everything else:

        ${fold { cons: \x y -> "- ${x}\n${y}", nil: "" } driftWarnings}

        Do not delete annotations to avoid a warning. Fewer annotations is
        a worse answer, not a safer one.
        "

in  prompt
      { key
      , text: "
          You are writing chase annotations for the module ${modName}.

          An annotation records what a maintainer cannot see from the type
          signature. The signature is already in the file; repeating it in
          English is worthless.

          These are real invariants from this project, and they are the
          standard to meet:

            renderCoverage :: Text -> Maybe [(Text, [TestRef])] -> [Text]
              ! Nothing -> [] (no coverage analysis ran; emit nothing)
              ! Just covMap with empty refs -> ['  ? no test references']

            preprocessCpp :: Text -> Text
              ! blanks rather than deletes: each stripped line becomes
                T.empty so line numbers in the resulting text match the
                original one-for-one
              ! preserves BOTH branches of #if/#else as live code

            buildTestIndex :: [TestBinding] -> TestRefIndex
              ! self-references (a binding referring to its own name) are
                filtered out before indexing
              ! duplicate refs from the same (module, function) are
                coalesced; the line of the FIRST occurrence wins

          Each one names something specific: a case and its result, a
          choice and its consequence, an ordering, a filter. None of them
          could be guessed from the type.

          These would all be rejected, and they are what a model writes
          when it has not read the body:

            ! acquire creates a connection pool using the provided DBConfig
            ! runSession executes a session within the provided connection pool
            ! renderDBError converts a DBError into a readable text message

          Here is the module's structural skeleton: every signature in the
          file, the imports, the data declarations, and the invariants
          already recorded for neighbouring functions.

          ${bundle}

          Here is the module's SOURCE, including the function bodies the
          skeleton omits. This is where the facts are. Read the body of a
          function before writing anything about it.

          ${source}

          Here is who CALLS each function in this module, derived by
          scanning every file in the project. This is fact, not guess. Use
          it: a function called from one place in the same module is an
          internal helper, one called from several modules is an
          interface, and one with no callers is either an entry point or
          dead.

          ${callers}

          For each top-level signature, emit at most one invariant whose
          'function' field exactly matches the signature name.

          The invariant's 'body' is a list of short factual statements
          drawn FROM THE BODY of that function: which other function it
          calls and with what arguments, which constant or setting it
          uses, what it does in the failing or empty case, what order
          things happen in, what it deliberately does not do. Name things.
          A statement with no identifier in it is usually a restatement.

          If the body gives you nothing beyond the type, emit no
          invariant for that function. An absent annotation is honest; a
          vacuous one is noise.

          Emit a decision only when the source contains a COMMENT that
          gives a reason for a choice. Quote that reason. If no comment
          gives a reason, emit no decisions: a rationale you worked out
          yourself is not the author's.

          Emit an open issue only for a hazard in code that EXISTS: a
          partial function, an unchecked assumption, a case that is
          silently dropped. Never write that something is missing, not
          implemented, or should do X. If the function is there, describe
          what it actually gets wrong or nothing at all.

          Prefer three specific invariants to nine vague ones.

          ${driftSection}
        "
      }
      : { invariants:
            List { function: Text
                 , body: List Text
                 }
        , decisions:
            List { name: Text
                 , what: Text
                 , why: Text
                 , affects: List Text
                 }
        , openIssues:
            List { name: Text
                 , what: Text
                 , why: Text
                 , blocking: List Text
                 , affects: List Text
                 }
        }